options.explanation:
$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}(x)\cdot y-x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(1)\cdot y-x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{y-x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}
\end{align}$

Now let's plug $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\dfrac{x}{y}}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{y-x\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{y-x\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{x}{y}}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{y^2-x^2}{y^3}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Finally, let's plug $\blueD{x=8}$ and $\maroonD{y=-6}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\left.\dfrac{\maroonD y^2-\blueD x^2}{\maroonD y^3}\right\rvert_{(\blueD{8},\maroonD{-6})}&=\dfrac{(\maroonD{-6})^2-(\blueD{8})^2}{(\maroonD{-6})^3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{7}{54}
\end{align}$
